  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $p,q$ be prime numbers. For how many values of $b$ does $x^2+bx+pq=0$ have integer solutions?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Four values: $b=\\pm(pq+1),\\pm(p+q)$, including when $p=q$.\n\nUse the same-sign integer factor pairs of $pq$.",
      "worked": "Let the integer roots be $r,s$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}rs&=pq\\\\b&=-(r+s).\\end{align*}$$\nSince $pq>0$, the roots have the same sign. The positive factor pairs are $(1,pq)$ and $(p,q)$; the negative pairs are $(-1,-pq)$ and $(-p,-q)$. Hence\n$$b=\\pm(pq+1),\\quad\\pm(p+q).$$\nThese are four distinct values, including when $p=q$, because $pq+1-(p+q)=(p-1)(q-1)>0$.\n\nTherefore, there are exactly four values of $b$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
